1. Hugging Face
Hugging Face is an AI platform and community that provides tools, libraries, pretrained models, and datasets for machine learning and Natural Language Processing (NLP). 
It allows developers and researchers to find, download, use, and train AI models. Its main tools include Transformers for loading pretrained models, Datasets for managing 
datasets, and Tokenizers for converting text into tokens. It also provides a platform called Spaces for sharing and demonstrating AI applications.
2. Unsloth
Unsloth is a library designed to make Large Language Model (LLM) fine-tuning faster and more memory-efficient. It helps reduce GPU memory consumption and training time while 
supporting techniques such as LoRA and QLoRA. It is useful when working with limited computing resources and integrates with the Hugging Face ecosystem.
3. LLM Model Fine-Tuning
3.1 Fine-Tuning (Page 4)
Fine-tuning is a technique used to adapt a pretrained model to a specific task by continuing its training on a smaller, task-specific dataset. Instead of training 
a model from scratch, we use the knowledge it has already learned and adjust its parameters to improve performance on the new task.
3.2 Model & Preprocessor Setup
A. Model Selection 
Model selection is the process of choosing an appropriate pretrained model for a particular task. The selected model will be used as the starting point for fine-tuning.
B. Model Loader (Pages 7–10)
A model loader is a utility or function that loads a pretrained model, its architecture, and its weights into memory for inference or further training (fine-tuning).

There are two main types of model loaders in Hugging Face Transformers:

1. Model-Specific Loaders: These loaders are designed for a particular model architecture. They are used when we already know which model we want to load, such as BERT, GPT-2, or T5. 
Examples include BertForSequenceClassification, GPT2LMHeadModel, and T5ForConditionalGeneration.

2. AutoModel Loaders: These loaders automatically select the correct model architecture based on the model identifier provided. They make loading models easier 
because we do not need to specify the architecture manually. Examples include AutoModelForSequenceClassification, AutoModelForTokenClassification, and AutoModelForCausalLM.
